ou should  google him check out his article and  also we have JD Knotts who's a grad  student in our lab who has other work  that he will be publishing in the coming  year that also stems from this project  and so I wanted to give them credit  before I talk about this because I have  learned a lot from them and they helped  get some of this work off the ground but  as I mentioned before we're really  interested in how these two parts of the  brain which we call DL PFC &amp; VT might  encode these images that people are  seeing which region is really critical  and has encoded the content that people  are seeing there's a lot of debate right  now in the scientific literature about  when we try to decode information from  different regions is that actually just  plain harder to decode from do PFC  because neurons and code things in more  complex ways so there's a great article  up on bio archive by Bhandari and a few  other authors I think David Audrey is  the senior author there and they say  look we know that in neuroscience we've  had success decoding from things like  occipital and ventral temporal areas but  if you look at the results in the  neuroimaging literature decoding from  frontal cortex is flat-out hard but we  have tried to get  this problem of the difficulty of  decoding from PFC by applying what's  called hyper alignment so there's an  effective technique for trying to align  brains across participants to more  effectively read out the content in  different regions and I know I'm running  a little low on time so I won't get into  all of the nitty-gritty details on what  hyper alignment is and how it works but  we have found fairly effective ways to  decode from PFC what I'm showing here  are just some pairwise classification  results for trying to decode whether or  not people saw one category versus  another category so if I want to know at  any given point in time whether or not  someone is seen say a snake versus a  rabbit or a dolphin versus a bat if  those are the two images I'm interested  in I found early on that you could  actually decode with a very high degree  of accuracy from parts of frontal cortex  so these accuracies I'm showing here are  becoming a giant sin in machine learning  they're both training and testing on the  same data set and there are reasons why  I'm showing this finger here for the  project I did and how we build decoders  don't believe that the y-axis on this  slide but know that the content is there  we have done analyses that are much less  sinful and much more correct in terms of  trying to decode information and we saw  early on in this box lock that I'm  showing here that you can decode the  category that people are seeing whether  or not you're decoding from BT those  posterior areas or PFC which is shown on  the right hand slide so early on we saw  that we can decode content for both the  posterior part of the brain and the  frontal part of the brain and I'm very  excited to say that in the last few  weeks I'm just gonna give a teaser here  and I won't actually show the data we  have been able to blow these decoding  accuracies out of the water in both of  these regions using hyper alignment and  trying to come up with better algorithms  to read out the perceptual content so  you can see on this slide from our early  results we can decode above a chance in  PFC and I can  for the last three weeks we are able to  decode with a very high degree of  accuracy from both of these areas using  hyper alignment so I have to end the  main scientific part of this talk with  just a teaser a little bit of data here  and the de teaser we are going to be  talking about these new techniques and  these new results at the assc conference  in poland in the coming year  and I should also be presenting this  later at the Society for Neuroscience  conference in 2018 in San Diego so for  those of you that are skeptical that  prefrontal cortex encodes perceptual  contents we have very interesting  results to show you that go even above  and beyond the data but I'm showing you  today which shows that we can decode  things above chance so there's ways that  we've tried to build on hyper alignment  and deep learning and a number of new  techniques of the field that worked very  well so let's take stock as this come to  a close you have seen today or at least  heard from me and hopefully I've  convinced you that sensory awareness and  your conscious sensory experience  involves confidence decisions  integration content so many different  things that all make up what it means to  have an experience and I think today  we've seen that some of these ideas that  we can dichotomize the brain in certain  ways to talk about where content resides  and we're essentially processes of  report and attention reside that might  be oversimplified things I I'm not  denying that the giant areas in red  shown here in this image are important  for perceptual experience I think  there's a tremendous amount of  scientific evidence that indicates that  yes that is the case but I would argue  that with a number of the findings I  show today and some of the findings that  I referenced as well there is increasing  evidence for a role of frontal cortical  areas in facilitating conscious sensory  experience and for those of you that are  interested I'll close by showing just a  couple images this has been a very  interesting debate we've been involved  with that was published in the Journal  of Neuroscience so if you're interested  you can look up these articles by  Melanie Joly and her co-authors as well  as myself and watch some videos online  of patients that have different types of  brain lesions  so sometimes you can see in patients  really vivid examples of how sensory  experience or function might change  based on where the lesion is so I  recommend checking out our discussions  of not only historical data but also  more contemporary patients and there are  some other pieces of evidence like the  one I'm showing here that if you dig  into that article in the Journal of  Journal neuroscience you might find  helpful so just to conclude today when  you think about what conscious  experience is like one of the metaphors  that people have used in literature is  the Cartesian theatre so it's almost  like when we look out at the world  there's a little man inside our head  that seems to have access to like a  movie screen the way the world is  rendered in front of us and my argument  today would be that when we think about  which parts of the brain facilitate the  content that's on that movie screen when  we look out at the world I know that  posterior areas do play a critical role  in facilitating the content that's on  the screen but I would argue that you  don't get a seat in the kinetic  Cartesian theater unless you have your  frontal lobes working and specifically  the dorsal lateral parts of your frontal  lobe so it's a fun debate that we're  involved in we were using a lot of  paradigms to try to investigate this  topic and I'm excited to see what  results people come up with in the  coming years to try to resolve this  debate about which parts of the brain  are necessary in getting us that seat in  the Cartesian theater so I'll close  today before we turn it over to the Q&amp;A  with just acknowledgments of a number of  people that have helped with this work  in the last five years  thanks to my postdoc advisor Hawk 1-mile  Michele bastos lab facilitated the the  early experiments that I talked about  this presentation Bob Knight was had  great discussions with him about that  Journal of Neuroscience article I  referenced and I probably can't go  through everyone else on this slide in  the way that I should in the time that I  have left but everyone that is listed  here know that I'm extremely thankful  for what it is that you contributed to  all of these projects would not have  been possible without all of their help  including all the names I have wedged in  the lower right hand side of this slide  I did my graduate work at UCLA and a  fantastic army of undergraduates to help  with everything so for even the small  names that are towards the lower right  thank you to all of my undergrads that  help with these projects to all of my  mentors and collaborators listed on the  slide and if you made it this far online  thank you so much for listening as well  so I'll turn it back now over to Tiffany  and hopefully we can have a brief Q&amp;A  before as we wrap things up here so  thanks again to all of you online and  thank you to all of you got lab roots  for organizing this well thank you dr.  Agard for that informative presentation  we will now start the live Q&amp;A portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question drop-down  box located on the far left of your  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on your screen  and click on the send button we will  answer as many questions as we have time  for today so let's get started with our  first question and this question asks  why would our perceptual systems have a  tendency towards making false alarms in  our periphery for object detection so  this is a great question that normally  when we think about our perceptual  systems we think of these in the context  of evolution right so evolution over a  period of hundreds of thousands if not  millions of years has been sort of  guiding how our perceptual systems work  and typically we think they're going to  be getting better and better at making  use of information in the sensory world  around us  so this experimental evidence that we  have for why we tend to make false  alarms seems counterintuitive you would  think that our perceptual systems would  be close to optimal with how they make  use of information and they're not so  that's a good question at this point all  we can do is speculate but I want to  actually reference a researcher by the  name of Michael Graciano here who  published a piece in the Atlantic  wrestling with why we might see some of  these interesting results when evolution  should have weeded them out and his idea  was there might be times where if we're  trying to survive and  dynamic and changing world that our  perceptual systems might be prone to  sort of have a trigger finger if you  will it might be prone to jump the gun  to try to say that something is there  even when it's not to spur action so  this could occur in the context of like  predator identification so our  perceptual systems might have a bias to  try to think things are out there even  when they're not because that could be  optimal and motivating are either  approach or avoidance of things that  might be dangerous and similarly for  things like like food or mates having a  little bit of a tendency to jump the gun  and think something is there might spur  action in a way that actually could be  optimal to enhance survival rather than  simply remaining stationary until we  have more information so I would say at  this point we're still only speculating  about the evolutionary reasons why that  might be in place but we're going to try  to conduct more experiments or a  laboratory to probe this question more  thoroughly to see how widely this  tendency for false alarms generalizes  across different contexts and as we ask  different types of questions so it's a  good one we have we have ideas for why  evolution might have put that in place  but we need more data before we can say  something conclusive about it excellent  thank you so much for your answer the  next question asks where do perceptual  priors come from and are they  established early in life through  sensory experience driven by genetics or  some combination of the two  another good difficult question and  where there's not a true consensus about  the answer so I mentioned before that  light from above prior and for those of  you that are listening online to this I  actually recommend that you google that  right now and there should be a couple  images that pop up that show you  vivid demonstrations of the light from  above prior and this has been an open  question about why that prior might be  there is it something where when we're  little kids we always experience lights  from above on the ceilings and we know  the Sun is above so maybe we have this  tendency to things light comes from  above just because that's the way it  always happens in our world or other  people think that you  as we have evolved over the course of  you know many many many years maybe  that's actually been built into our  systems inherently to interpret the  perceptual world in this way and I had a  conversation with someone who claims all  references Brian Shaw from Yale  University told me this story about he  was as a graduate student digging in the  library in the stacks one night looking  for another article and he told me he  came across this interesting experiment  where they raised chickens in the 60s  from an environment where the lights  always came from below so someone about  40 or 50 years ago actually tried to  conduct an experiment with baby chicks  and they asked okay if I always raise  animals in a way where the light comes  from the other direction and I show them  these ambiguous stimuli where we usually  see because of the light from above  prior things are indented in certain  ways he said that in this article they  showed that even when the light always  came from below they still seemed to  have a light from above prior so this is  actually one example that it seems as  though even if you change someone's life  experience or you change the life  experience of an animal it still seems  like the light from above prior is still  present so I would love to see that old  study replicated in in some ways because  I think it would be fascinating to have  a more modern data to speak to this  question but I actually come from the  stance of I think it's actually  something that now is built-in in  genetics there's no doubt that priors  can change based on sensory experience  but it's a question to how much they can  change and right now I tend to think  that our perceptual systems tend to have  a way of being wired that lead us  towards these biases but that's another  question where we need more data to  answer it conclusively so but it's a  good one well excellent thank you once  again our last question of the day asks  what our future directions for your  research on prefrontal cortex  now that you have shown shown you can  decode object categories effectively  from using from this area using FM  are I excited about of all of the things  I'm involved with right now in research  there are so many times we debate like  what consciousness is that it's such a  fun topic but it never really feels like  we pin things down conclusively so like  everyone from philosophers to  neuroscientists to laypeople it's such a  cool question about what is  consciousness and now that we have  better techniques in fMRI I really want  to do two things so one is to try to  read out more vivid types of perceptual  content so if you take say the visual  domain and you break it up into things  like hue which is a sort of a synonym  for color or saturation or value you can  google what those being in terms of like  HSV space and I want to see whether or  not we can decode some of those aspects  of experience that we've always  attributed to visual areas so like can  we decode color from prefrontal cortex  can we decode spatial frequency now that  the algorithms are better now that the  decoding techniques are better I want to  show that that information is there so  we've done it with object categories  like I mentioned in my talk I want to  show that we can do it for these other  dimensions and then secondly what I'm  trying to do in some experiments right  now is to use what's called  neurofeedback to change neuro  representations in PFC and I want to see  if that can change your perceptual  experience in a given moment so there  are techniques that we can use now where  you can lie in a scanner and using  neurofeedback I can change patterns of  activity in certain parts of the brain  over time and what I want to demonstrate  is a causal role for being able to  change neural patterns in PFC and have  that directly affect your perceptual  experience in a given moment because I  think what this area is really missing  right now is a conclusive causal  demonstration that if you change  activity in PFC it affects what you see  in any given moment we know from say  surgeries on epileptic patients where if  we insert electrodes into the posterior  areas of their brain and we actually  stimulate those electrodes it can cause  visual  nations so that's really informative and  interesting from a scientific standpoint  because it provides evidence that parts  of posterior areas can actually result  in changing a conscious content that you  experience so there's a great paper in  PNAS by I think it's goin shulk and  Nancy kanwisher where they show that in  epileptic patients if you stimulate  certain posterior areas the people start  hallucinating rainbows and hallucinating  faces in objects and that's a great  demonstration that these areas play  critical roles and what you see in the  given moments and what I would like to  do is to use other neurofeedback  techniques to try to show that if you  change something about neurons with PFC  you're actually modifying the content  because I think in this idea that PFC  plays a critical role in conscious  experience it's those types of  scientific demonstrations that would be  most informative to demonstrate a role  for this area so that's where my  research is going right now in the  coming year and if you keep an eye on my  Google Scholar page or my lab website  I'll updates throughout the coming year  hopefully you can stay abreast of the  new results that I have to see what new  things were coming out with from the lab  excellent well thank you so much I would  like to thank dr. on guard for his  presentation I would also like to thank  leopards for making today's educational  webcast possible before we go I want to  let everyone know that today's webcast  will be available for on-demand viewing  through June of 2018 you will receive an  email from laberd's letting you know  when the webcast will be available for  replay please share this announcement  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event that's all for now  thank you for joining us and we hope to  see you here again soon at lab roots  goodbye
 

